 ],
 "literal": "… vi / making and eating fiber and bark strip, together / to the very large family who suffered as hunger consumed them / having searched the directions with abundant food as their target / sweating, suffering, withering, their sides perishing, having come / in that place where the harsh voice of the owl of the desert path resembling a drum / echoes with the calling voice of the female of the kite perched in the beautiful fork of the sirissa tree / in the long path of intense drought where the bamboo is dried and withered / leaving behind the forest where the striped bowstring hemp is shriveled / like birds seeking a fruit-bearing tree / as if bright weapons poured like a rain of falling fruit / spreading the gushing and rising blood upon the ground / harvesting the ripened rich ears of grain, heaping the stalks / gathering and scattering the many heaps of fallen corpses / wielding sword-blades as if they were the leaves of the palmyra, with male elephants as bulls / on the threshing floor threshed by the male buffalo where men fall / beating the wide-surfaced taṭāri drum so it sounds clearly / praising, saying 'may the wide field of fierce victory flourish!' / O great lord, I came for the mountain-resembling gift of lofty beautiful tusks fitted with iron tips / O lord of the battlefield of terrifying expanse / where the red-eared vultures wander with vultures / as the weeping-voiced demoness dances, stuffing the striped intestines mixed with the flesh of the group / raising her large hands with armlets, not fearing those who lie pierced as the sharp-practiced spears leaped!",
 "translation": "O Lord of the battlefield of terrifying expanse! We came to you, O great lord, seeking the mountain-like gift of lofty, beautiful elephant tusks fitted with iron tips. We are a large family of relatives who once survived by eating only fiber and bark strips, suffering as hunger consumed us. We wandered through all directions searching for food. Sweating, suffering, and withering, with our bodies nearly perishing, we traveled through the long, dry desert paths where bamboos are parched and striped bowstring hemp is shriveled, where the harsh drum-like hooting of the desert owl echoes with the calling of the female kite perched on the beautiful fork of a sirissa tree. Leaving that dry forest behind, we sought you like birds seeking a fruit-bearing tree. On your battlefield, bright weapons poured down like a rain of falling fruit, spreading gushing blood upon the ground. You harvested the fallen corpses like rich ears of grain, heaping them up, and threshed them on the field where men fell, using male elephants as bulls and wielding swords as blades. Beating our wide-surfaced taṭāri drums, we praised your fierce victory, wishing that your wide field may flourish! In this terrifying place, red-eared vultures wander alongside other vultures, and a weeping demoness dances, stuffing herself with striped intestines mixed with flesh, raising her armleted hands over the brave warriors who lie fallen, pierced by sharp spears.",
 "modern": "வறுமையால் நாரையும் மரப்பட்டையையும் தின்று பசியால் வாடிய சுற்றத்தாருடன், உணவு தேடி திசையெங்கும் அலைந்து, களைத்து, மெலிந்து, சாகும் நிலையை அடைந்து வந்தோம். கள்ளி மரங்களும் மூங்கில்களும் காய்ந்துபோன, பருந்தும் ஆந்தையும் அலறும் வறண்ட பாலைவனத்தைக் கடந்து, பழங்கள் நிறைந்த மரத்தைத் தேடிச் செல்லும் பறவைகளைப் போல உன்னை நாடி வந்தோம். போர்க்களத்தில் ஆயுதங்கள் மழைபோலப் பொழிய, குருதி நிலத்தில் பரவியது. நெற்கதிர்களை அறுத்து குவிப்பது போல, பகைவர்களின் உடல்களைக் குவித்து, யானைகளைப் பிணைகளாகக் கொண்டு, வாள்களைக் கொண்டு போர்க்களத்தில் போரடித்தாய். அத்தகைய போர்க்களத்தைப் புகழ்ந்து, எமது தடாரிப் பறையை முழக்கி, இரும்பு பூண் அணிந்த பெரிய யானைத் தந்தங்களைப் பரிசாகப் பெற வந்தோம். கூர்மையான வேல்களால் குத்தப்பட்டு வீழ்ந்த வீரர்களின் உடல்களுக்கு இடையே, பேய்மகள் குடல்களைத் தின்று நடனமாட, கழுகுகள் வட்டமிடும் அச்சம் தரும் போர்க்களத்தின் தலைவனே!",
 "explanation": "The poet, Ūṉpoti Pacuṅkuṭaiyār, addresses the Chola king Iḷañcēṭceṉṉi, praising his victory on the battlefield. The poet describes the extreme poverty of his family, who had to survive on tree bark and fiber, and how they traveled through a harsh, dry desert landscape to reach the king. This desert journey is described with vivid imagery of parched bamboos, shriveled hemp, and the eerie calls of owls and kites. The poet compares their arrival to birds seeking a fruit-bearing tree, highlighting the king's generosity. The battlefield is described using agricultural metaphors, where the slaughter of enemies is depicted as harvesting grain, and the trampling of corpses by elephants is shown as threshing. The poem belongs to the Vākai tiṇai, celebrating victory, and specifically the Maṟakkaḷavaḻi tuṟai, which describes the battlefield as a threshing floor.",
